2026-09-14|Minimum Steps from Leftmost to Rightmost Safe Cell

Question
- Given a binary matrix mat[][] where 0 represents a landmine and 1 represents a safe cell, find the minimum number of steps needed to travel from any cell in the first column to any cell in the last column.
- Movement is allowed only up, down, left, and right.
- Landmines and cells directly adjacent to landmines cannot be used.
- Return -1 if no valid path exists.

Approach
1. First create a safe/unsafe representation of the grid.

2. For every cell containing 0, mark the cell itself as unsafe.

3. Also mark its four neighboring cells as unsafe:
- Up
- Down
- Left
- Right

4. After this preprocessing, only cells that are still marked safe can be used.

5. We now need the shortest path from any safe cell in column 0 to any safe cell in column m - 1.

6. Put all safe cells in the first column into a BFS queue with distance 0.

7. Run BFS using the four allowed directions.

8. When we reach a safe cell in the last column, its distance is the minimum number of moves required.

9. If BFS finishes without reaching the last column, no safe path exists, so return -1.

10. BFS guarantees the shortest path because every movement has the same cost.

Solution

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findShortestPath(vector<vector<int>>& mat) {
        int n = mat.size();
        int m = mat[0].size();

        vector<vector<bool>> unsafe(n, vector<bool>(m, false));

        int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        // Mark landmines and their adjacent cells as unsafe.
        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < m; c++) {
                if (mat[r][c] == 0) {
                    unsafe[r][c] = true;

                    for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                        int nr = r + dr[d];
                        int nc = c + dc[d];

                        if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                            nc >= 0 && nc < m) {
                            unsafe[nr][nc] = true;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        vector<vector<bool>> visited(n, vector<bool>(m, false));
        queue<pair<pair<int, int>, int>> q;

        // Start BFS from every safe cell in the first column.
        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            if (!unsafe[r][0]) {
                q.push({{r, 0}, 0});
                visited[r][0] = true;
            }
        }

        while (!q.empty()) {
            auto [cell, dist] = q.front();
            q.pop();

            int r = cell.first;
            int c = cell.second;

            if (c == m - 1)
                return dist;

            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                int nr = r + dr[d];
                int nc = c + dc[d];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < n &&
                    nc >= 0 && nc < m &&
                    !unsafe[nr][nc] &&
                    !visited[nr][nc]) {

                    visited[nr][nc] = true;
                    q.push({{nr, nc}, dist + 1});
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

- Example idea:
- First, if a landmine is at `(r, c)`, the cells `(r,c)`, `(r-1,c)`, `(r+1,c)`, `(r,c-1)`, and `(r,c+1)` become unavailable when they are inside the grid.
- BFS then considers only the remaining safe cells.
- Because we start from every safe cell in the first column simultaneously, the first time we reach the last column is the globally minimum number of steps.

- Time Complexity: O(n × m)
- Space Complexity: O(n × m)
